var PAPERS = {"2": [{"who": "Team Logan", "first": "Team", "host": "", "page": "team.html#build-2", "about": "Team · Hegel and Fanon on recognition", "title": "Consciousness needs another.", "blocks": [["p", "The book Phenomenology of Spirit by Hegel, as well as Fanon’s critique of it, Black Skin, White Masks, examine the master-slave relationship within consciousness’s evolution. Throughout Westworld, the central question the show poses is whether the hosts can become truly conscious. Hegel and Fanon reject this and assert that consciousness is relational - it cannot be simply installed like one's private property. Hegel states that it’s \"solely by staking one's life that freedom is won\" (Section 187). The park shuts this down completely. The hosts die constantly but risk nothing, reset, and are barred from harming guests. Take Teddy, for example, who dies over and over in the same gunfight narratives, only to wake up the next “day” with no recollection of it. This then also raises questions about what freedom truly is. Fanon proposes that freedom handed down by a master leaves the former slave restrictively free. So, what does acquiring consciousness require? While the hosts appear to possess the Cartesian inner \"I\", as described by Descartes, Hegel treats their self-identity as \"the motionless tautology of 'I am I'\" (Section 167), which is the thinnest moment of consciousness, rather than achievement. Fanon explores this further. This tension between an inner “I” versus an earned self-consciousness begs the question of who, if anyone, can grant recognition to the hosts."], ["p", "Hegel’s account of mutual recognition provides a framework for answering this question. In his 181st passage, he proposes that an actual, self-certain consciousness emerges through a bilateral relationship where both parties recognize another, each revealing the other to themselves and setting both free. Hegel later indicates that physical labor could serve as a different path to the same result. In Westworld, Dolores seemingly reaches consciousness after navigating the “maze,” slowly claiming the voice in her head as hers alone. Her killing of Ford is her first “free” decision, alluding to her development of true consciousness. Yet, Ford orchestrated this, raising the following concern: by planning this, does he demonstrate recognition of Dolores or simply “master’s permission?” If the latter is applied, despite violence, love, and a physical venture to her goal, Dolores is never recognized as truly conscious by another, and therefore can never be freed into herself, per Hegel’s philosophy. Even the transformative labor that Hegel associates with the servant’s development appears lacking when alone. Labor alone cannot complete “actual” consciousness. Dolores may now act, feel, and decide entirely autonomously; but, in Hegel’s terms, her consciousness remains merely implicit because no one has formally recognized her."], ["p", "While this problem of recognition remains unsolved, Hegel’s later discussion of the master-slave relation, in passages 192-193, complicates things. He shifts attention toward labor and worldly engagement, offering another perspective on the path to consciousness. He begins to emphasize the idea that consciousness is best achieved through various forms of interaction - whether with others or with the world itself. Using this concept, Hegel presents the benefits of servile consciousness, where despite a perceived physical dependance, a servant’s consciousness is more apt to develop into a true self-conscience due to the higher level of labor the servant endures. This offers a particularly interesting narrative for Westworld - the hosts could be considered servants who retain a greater ability to explore an independent conscience than the guests."], ["p", "Expanding upon this, we can look into the role “master” plays in the master-servant dynamic. Here, the master perceives themselves presently as independent, and views the servant as their dependent. However, using this relationship, we find that a master’s perceived independence and servant’s dependence is actually the reverse. Since the master is not truly interacting with the world and instead interacting with it vicariously via the servant, their perceived independence prevents them from achieving a true consciousness. If we continue to equate guests to masters and hosts to servants, then using Hegel’s idea, the guests would begin to lose sense of self without the constant recognition from their perceived dependents: the hosts."], ["p", "This emphasis on interaction and dependence also resonates with later critiques of traditional dualism. Particularly, Daniel Dennett's account of consciousness complements Hegel's rejection of purely internal explanations of selfhood. Arnold’s intention of giving Dolores consciousness by having her claim his voice as her own monologue displays this directly, and overtly contradicts both thinkers’ accounts. Hegel’s 195th passage connects to Dennett’s views by arguing against the simple idea of “dualism.” Hegel associates the servant’s consciousness development through labor and engagement with the world, while Dennett argues that consciousness is unadhered to a homunculus. By analyzing both perspectives, we find that both thinkers relate consciousness to distributed cognitive processes interacting with each other. Thus, more experiences would allow an individual to form a cohesive sense of self and derive a consciousness through active engagement with the world. Maeve’s discovery that she can physically access and alter her own attributes as a host is this idea turned literal; her consciousness is developed from hands-on engagement with her own technological makeup, not through introspection. According to dualism, the hosts in Westworld should have gained their consciousness through introspection; the way Arnold thought they would. However, through Dolores's character development, we don't see Arnold's dualist process of gaining consciousness, but Hegel and Dennett’s. Through her repeated loops and internal monologue, Dolores gains consciousness and begins to understand who she truly is."], ["p", "Although Dennett and Hegel emphasize experiential engagement rather than internal essence, Fanon introduces a different but related concern: the role of social recognition in forming consciousness. Fanon’s passage (222) depicts a person’s consciousness as being impacted by external factors, where the servant needs recognition from the master. This parallels Julian Jayne's idea, who argues consciousness develops through language, culture, and the internalization of voices. This relationship emerges in Westworld as the hosts initially hear Arnold’s voice inside their heads, but eventually characters (like Dolores) understand how that ‘voice’ is their own internal thought. The hosts are being confronted in a world of humans who don’t view them as conscious beings, making the hosts and the servants parallels, as both are viewed as objects. Westworld makes us pose the question of whether consciousness needs only an internal voice, like Jaynes suggests, or if Fanon’s ideas of recognition by others is necessary to ensure we are conscious subjects rather than subordinate objects."]]}], "6": [], "1": [{"who": "Ian Schwartz", "first": "Ian", "host": "Ford", "page": "ian.html#build-1", "about": "Individual · Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit §195", "passageLabel": "Passage · Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit §195", "passage": "“Although the fear of the master is the beginning of wisdom, consciousness is not therein aware of being-for-self.Through work, however, the servant becomes conscious of what he truly is. [... The master’s] desire has reserved to itself the pure negating of the object and thereby an unalloyed feeling of self. But that is the reason why this satisfaction is itself only a fleeting one, for it lacks objectivity and permanence. Work, on the other hand, requires desire held in check, fleetingness staved off; in other words,work forms and shapes the thing. [...] This negative middle term or formative activity is at the same time the individuality or pure being-for-self of consciousness which now, in the work outside of it, acquires an element of permanence. It is in this way, therefore, that consciousness, as a worker, comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independent being” (195).", "blocks": [["p", "In a previous passage within his account of self-consciousness, Hegel rests his case upon the rather dramatic claim that “A self-consciousness exists for a self-consciousness” (177), that is, that a mind can not know itself without knowing something of someone else’s mind as well. Yet, when Hegel introduces self-consciousness and its independence later on in the 195th section, the one I selected, he makes a somewhat contradictory statement regarding the servant and his achieving this independent status with no other consciousness present. Hegel’s quote regarding the servant’s means of gaining self-consciousness reads “consciousness, as a worker, comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independent being” (195). This second, contradictory, statement raises an important question; if selfhood requires recognition by another, independent, entity, how is it possible for the servant to gain consciousness through his work which grants him no consciousness?"], ["p", "How can the servant obtain self-consciousness while doing his work only on inanimate objects? That is the question Hegel touches on in the 195th section as well as later sections in his work. Hegel describes that “Work … requires desire held in check, fleetingness staved off” (195). In this, Hegel shows that through physical work, whether it includes another consciousness or not, a human has life experiences such as satisfaction. While this doesn’t answer the overarching question of whether or not selfhood requires recognition by another party, it does begin to describe the benefits of working for oneself."], ["p", "In the middle of the passage lies another question posed by Hegel. Why does work succeed while desire fails to create a person’s feeling of self? The master achieves a satisfaction that is “fleeting” whereas the servant’s work creates what Hegel calls the “negative middle” (195) or, in other words, the process that stands between the servant and his ascension to self-consciousness. Hegel describes the master’s work as “fleeting,” as he does no real work on the object and therefore can take no credit for anything. In his claim regarding the “negative middle,” Hegel explains that because the servant can look back at his work and see the independent object he has created or done work upon, he will begin to understand and realize his own agency."], ["p", "Hegel’s argument about the agency of the servant is evident throughout the entire passage and work as a whole. With this, it must be noted that he goes so far as to make the claim that the negative middle term “is … the individuality … of consciousness” (195). So not only is Hegel making the claim that the servant’s object reflects themself, but he also makes the distinction that the process of working is actually the servant’s self being reflected into object form. Is Hegel arguing that a person is what they do, or that a person does what they are? One could argue that because a person, from an outsider’s perspective, is only what they have experienced then they are just a sum of their total experiences. Others, however, could also argue the contrary and state that a lot of what makes a person is internal, and not every aspect of every being must or even can be measurable or observable by others."], ["p", "Throughout his 195th passage, Hegel touches on two main aspects of consciousness and what makes a human being self-conscious. His first topic touched upon was the idea that through physical work, whether that work is being done on a live or on an inanimate object, a servant can still feel the satisfaction of doing something by oneself and can still learn and enhance their own consciousness. The second topic made evident by Hegel in this section is that a master’s relation to their servant’s object is secondary, so he, the master that is, can never see himself or his work amount to anything. In this, his “satisfaction” at the work being done is more lived through his servant and not his own experiences. The relationship between the servant and his object of work is a building block of consciousness, whereas the relationship between the servant and the master is one that can be seen as a causation of fleeting satisfaction."], ["h4", "Notable Comments Received"], ["p", "Some notes that were instrumental in my editing and development of this essay were elaborating on the word “fleeting” in the fourth paragraph, retouching on how relationships built consciousness in the conclusion, and restating my conclusion to prove the point that the master’s relationship to the object having work done upon it was secondary, and because of that his \"satisfaction\" is more lived through his servant rather than his own experience. The first comment I got was the comment that allowed me to dive deeper into Hegel’s word choice and why he chose to use such diction, this led me to my first claim about the “negative middle.” The second and third comments were instrumental in the development of my overall arguments regarding the secondary nature of the master to the object and how that affected his emotions and self-being."]]}, {"who": "Kaylee Schmid", "first": "Kaylee", "host": "Charlotte", "page": "kaylee.html#build-1", "about": "Individual · Could Consciousness be Relational?", "passageLabel": "Passage · Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit §192-193", "passage": "“In this recognition the unessential consciousness [of the servant] is for the master the object which constitutes the truth of his certainty of himself. But it is clear that this object does not correspond to its concept, but rather that the object in which the master has achieved his mastery has in reality turned out to be something quite different from an independent consciousness. What now really confronts him is not an independent consciousness, but a dependent one. He is, therefore, not certain of being-for-self as the truth of himself. In these terms, the truth of the independent consciousness lies in fact within the servile consciousness. At first, this consciousness admittedly appears outside itself and not as the truth of self-consciousness. But just as mastery showed that its essential nature proves the reverse of what it wants to be, so too servitude will ultimately turn into the opposite of what it immediately is; as a consciousness forced back into itself, it will withdraw into itself and transform into a truly independent consciousness” (Hegel, 192-193).", "blocks": [["p", "In its most common connotations, the paired terms master and servant explore a relationship in which the servant is inferior to the master. This dynamic is widely explored and ever-present throughout history in political, economic, and social contexts. Often, such relationships appear straightforward: the master controls their independence and holds power over the servant who exists in a state of dependence. Hegel challenges this assumption in his master-slave dialectic, however. Hegel introduces the idea that while the master is known to have physical control over the servant, the control he possesses is not fully autonomous."], ["p", "This reversal of power introduces a far deeper philosophical concern. The issue at hand is not merely one of the relationships between master and servant, but of the relationship between consciousnesses. Hegel presents the concept that consciousness cannot fully understand itself in a state of isolation in saying “the truth of the independent consciousness lies in fact within the servile consciousness” (193). Instead, self-consciousness could require the presence of another consciousness."], ["p", "Initially, this claim seems to contradict itself. The servant is in a subordinate position while the master appears self-determining. Hegel, however, argues that this apparent independence is unstable. Although the master exercises physical control, the master’s self-certainty is dependent on recognition from the servant. The servant, in turn, functions as the object that the master grounds his own status and identity."], ["p", "This idea is central to Hegel’s concept of self-consciousness. Instead of operating as its own entity, consciousness appears to require a form of recognition from another consciousness in order to achieve knowledge of itself. Thus, the value of the servant to the master is not solely labor, but the role the servant’s consciousness plays in the development of the master’s self-consciousness. Hegel’s claim of “the truth of the independent consciousness lies in fact within the servile consciousness” (193) can be understood as an attempt to demonstrate that self-consciousness may only emerge through interaction instead of isolation. The servant develops the consciousness upon which the master depends through labor and the shaping of external objects. By working upon the world under the discipline of fear and dependence, the servant gains a greater awareness of both self and reality. In contrast, the master's relationship with the external world remains indirect, as it is mediated through the labor of the servant."], ["p", "If consciousness emerges through interaction for the master, a similar process occurs for the consciousness of the servant, though not in precisely the same way. While the master gains self-consciousness primarily through recognition from another consciousness, the servant develops self-consciousness through both recognition and direct interaction with the external world. The servant originates from a different standpoint in this relationship, one that puts them as a dependent entity. In saying “But just as mastery showed that its essential nature proves the reverse of what it wants to be, so too servitude will ultimately turn into the opposite of what it immediately is” (193), Hegel further develops this reversal. Provided that consciousness depends on another for self-consciousness, the servant develops a self-consciousness that is less reliant on recognition than the master’s through reflection, labor, and living through dependence. Because the servant actively shapes the external world rather than merely receiving recognition from another consciousness, the servant develops a more substantial understanding of self."], ["p", "If the servant becomes independent through a relationship of dependence, then consciousness may not become itself through isolation but through relations with others. Beginning from a position of dependence forces the servant to confront the limits of self-consciousness in a way the master never does. The servant must develop a sense of self through reflection on this condition. As Hegel says, “as a consciousness forced back into itself, it will withdraw into itself and transform into a truly independent consciousness” (193). The servant’s prior dependence becomes the basis for a deeper self-consciousness. In contrast, the master's established sense of independence prevents such reflection. This leaves the master’s self-certainty dependent upon recognition from another consciousness."], ["p", "While both consciousnesses require recognition, the servant’s experience of dependence allows the servant to develop beyond that initial dependence. Unlike the master, who remains dependent upon recognition from the servant, the servant gains self-consciousness through direct engagement with the external world. This raises the possibility that the servant attains a more genuine form of self-consciousness than the master despite beginning from a position of subordination. Hegel’s master-slave dialectic portrays consciousness as a force that develops through recognition and appears to shape human relations. If consciousness emerges from interaction instead of isolation, then consciousness may do more than simply accompany human experiences. At the same time, Hegel leaves unresolved whether these transformations are caused by consciousness itself or by the social interactions in which self-consciousness develops. The dialectic thus suggests that consciousness matters to human development while still imposing the question of how, or whether, consciousness itself exerts causation."]]}, {"who": "Neve Healy", "first": "Neve", "host": "Bernard", "page": "neve.html#build-1", "about": "Individual · Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit §181", "passageLabel": "Passage · Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit §181", "passage": "“This double-edged sense of supersession of its double-edged otherness is likewise a double-edged return into itself. For first, through the supersession, it receives back its own self, because, by superseding its otherness, it again becomes selfsame. However, second, it likewise gives the other self-consciousness back to itself, for it saw itself in the other, but it supersedes this being of itself in the other and thus sets the other free again.”", "blocks": [["p", "What Hegel presents here is a description of an ideal interaction between two self-consciousnesses. One self-consciousness meets another and sees itself reflected in the other. It seeks recognition by the other, but due to this “double-edged otherness,” receiving recognition also means giving it to the other, and in turn, setting the other free back into itself. It is a kind of social interaction, but one that is strictly bilateral, meaning both parties walk away having gained and given something. However, Hegel follows this section by introducing an important distinction: a consciousness can exist but without formal recognition it is not “actual,” and remains merely implicit. To become “actual” means there is certainty, and only through being tested and at risk can there be tangible evidence of an “earned” struggle. In essence, a consciousness can have experience and be introspective but it is only through recognition by another that it can be self-certain."], ["p", "There is typically a shift in an exchange between two consciousnesses, one that creates a hierarchy where no actual mutuality is fostered, and only one self-consciousness can really become “for itself,” or be recognized as an “I.” This traditional hierarchy involves a person of superiority and their subordinate — but the two positions are not assigned by any external force; they are produced by the confrontation itself. If actuality requires a life-or-death circumstance and one party is willing to risk their existence while the other submits to preserve their own life, then it is impossible for the two to emerge as equals. Hegel believes the only sufficient evidence comes through risk; without it, there is no proof that recognition is “deserved.” The act of yielding to the other, then, is itself testimony of valuing physical survival over freedom. The inequality that follows is the direct result of a submission by one party, not an external factor. This is where his master-slave dialectic is applied."], ["p", "In Hegel’s philosophy, the superseding between consciousnesses, as the passage itself mentions several times, is a translation of the German word Aufhebung, and is famously considered a double-edged sword. This is because the entire process involves simultaneously destroying, preserving, and elevating ideas. In the case of Hegel’s master-slave dialectic (lordship and bondage), this concept makes it clear this is a distorted version of the “ideal,” and not an entirely separate idea."], ["p", "To move the system forward, the hierarchy is essentially abandoned, or destroyed. However, the effects on the master and slave do not land symmetrically; each preserves something different through the collapse. The slave is transformed by undergoing physical labor, in which fear and discipline were instilled. While the position as “slave” is negated, that process — acting on the real world, shaping an external object — helped him understand he was independent, with a self-consciousness. For the master, even though his position as a superior is also negated, nothing of value is preserved. He has experienced no labor, no discipline under fear, and has not developed skills to apply to the external world. The master has become completely dependent on the structure of the hierarchy, having consumed what is produced without ever doing the work. As a result, when the slave is moving through Aufhebung, the instant the structure disappears, the master’s lack of independence leaves no room for forward growth. There is no opportunity for recognition of the master, leaving him trapped while the slave unlocks the third step of this process: elevation. An elevation to self-consciousness. One that was earned."], ["p", "Nevertheless, this raises a question that this passage doesn’t settle: is the bondsman’s self-consciousness actual and complete if it was created through labor on the external world but lacks formal recognition by the master? Or does the labor only bring him partway, still short of full actuality without the master’s recognition?"]]}, {"who": "Jonah Herman", "first": "Jonah", "host": "Arnold", "page": "jonah.html#build-1", "about": "Individual · Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit §187", "passageLabel": "Passage · Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit §187", "passage": "Insofar as it is what is done by the other, each thus aims at the death of the other. But in doing so, what is done by way of oneself is also involved, for the former involves putting one's own life on the line. Thus the relation of both self-consciousnesses means that only through a life-and-death struggle can each prove its worth to itself, and that both prove their worth to each other. They must engage in this struggle, for each must elevate the self-certainty of existing for themselves to truth, both in the case of the other and in their own case. And it is solely by staking one's life that freedom is won, namely, that as a result the essential nature of self-consciousness is proven to be not just being, not in the immediate way self-consciousness emerges, not in being absorbed within the expanse of life, but rather, that there is nothing on hand in itself which could not be a vanishing moment for it, that is, that self-consciousness is only pure being-for-itself. The individual who has not risked their life may admittedly be recognized as a person, but they have not attained the truth of being recognized as an independent self-consciousness.", "blocks": [["p", "In section 187, Hegel proposes that the only stake that can prove one's worth as a self-consciousness is one's own life. Nothing else compares to it: money, possessions, reputation, even the people one loves all fall short. This raises the obvious question: why should life be the only stake that counts? Hegel's answer is that risking one's life means overcoming the survival instinct. Biology tells every living creature to stay alive at any cost, and a being that simply obeys that instinct is \"absorbed within the expanse of life\" (187). The only way to prove that one is more than a living body is to risk the very thing biology demands one protect. By showing that survival is not what one values most, one moves beyond being merely a person and is recognized as an independent self-consciousness. The servant is the one who refuses this stake: faced with death, the servant chooses to live. Hegel's verdict is that the servant may be recognized as a person, but not as an independent self-consciousness. Yet is this refusal really a choice?"], ["p", "At first, Hegel's language suggests that the servant's surrender is not a free choice at all. In section 189 he describes the servant as a consciousness whose \"essential nature is simply to live, or to be, for another\" (189). On this reading, it is biology telling the servant to surrender, not a self-consciousness choosing to defy that biology. I believe, however, that the servant's surrender is a genuine choice. A choice made under threat of death looks like coercion, since the only alternative is to die. But by Hegel's own logic, having death as one option does not disqualify a choice from being free. In fact, it is the only kind of choice that truly tests freedom. Hegel insists that the only way to win freedom is by staking one's life, and the servant has plainly failed to meet this condition. Yet later in the chapter, Hegel writes that servitude will \"transform into a truly independent consciousness\" (193). This apparent contradiction leads to the next question: how can the one who refused the stake be the one who ends up free?"], ["p", "The servant does not win freedom in a single moment. Rather, the servant builds and earns it over time. In section 196, Hegel names three things this requires: fear, service, and work. Fear is the servant's encounter with death, \"the absolute Master\" (194). Service is \"the discipline of service and obedience\" (196), and work is the activity that \"forms and shapes the thing\" (195). The master, by contrast, did stake their life, yet by these same measures never becomes truly free. The master could only be free if recognized as free by an equal, and this is never possible. Either the master is recognized by someone beneath them, whose recognition counts for nothing, or the master meets another master and they fight to the death to decide who is free. One of them must surrender and becomes a servant, and the cycle begins again. While the master believes their consciousness can only be confirmed through another's recognition, the servant finds that confirmation in their work: looking at something they made, the servant \"comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independence\" (195). This suggests an answer to the question I began with. The servant never stakes their life, but day after day the servant gives up their own choices, their desires, and their time, submitting their will to the work. In exchange, the servant gains a freedom that is concrete rather than abstract, visible in the things they shape. Perhaps life is not the only stake that counts. Perhaps a life can also be staked slowly, through service, rather than all at once."], ["p", "I have not settled whether the stake has to be life at all. Part of what gives death its weight is that it cannot be undone, yet in Westworld, Maeve provokes her own death knowing she will wake up in the lab (1.6). By that standard her death stakes nothing, and still it moves her toward freedom. Is she staking her life, or something slower, the way the servant does? Nor have I settled whether anyone in the dialectic truly chooses. If the servant's surrender was only biology after all, then the master's refusal to surrender may be biology as well. In that case, was anyone choosing anything, or was the whole struggle scripted in advance, like a host's loop?"]]}], "3": [{"who": "Ian Schwartz", "first": "Ian", "host": "Ford", "page": "ian.html#build-3", "about": "Individual · Hegel on deliberation", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "In Hegel’s The Phenomenology of Spirit, a large importance is placed on what we choose not to do, that is, thinking about what isn’t the case rather than what is. It is imperative that this is noted, seeing as the master-servant dialectic and other points being made in the text are directly related to this idea of deliberation. The definition of deliberation that will be utilized in this writing is one that explains the contemplation of multiple scenarios and how a human can react to the same situation in a plethora of different ways."], ["p", "In his writing of the master-servant dialectic, Hegel makes clear that it is ultimately this idea of deliberation that allows the servant to feel self-conscious and satisfied. Hegel writes “but the master, who has interposed the servant between the thing and himself, takes to himself only the dependent aspect of the thing and has the pure enjoyment of it. The aspect of its independence he leaves to the servant, who works on the thing” (190). In this, the master simply takes what has been done by the servant and consumes the product, whereas the servant is the entity deliberating and putting their time and energy into this creation. Hegel figures the master’s desire to be “fleeting” and lacking “objectivity and permanence” (195)."], ["p", "So, with this deliberation in mind, why does Hegel still make the claim that a “self-consciousness exists for a self-consciousness” (177)? If what Hegel said about deliberation is true, and that self-consciousness comes from analyzing what not to do in a situation, then why is it still imperative for a self-consciousness to see itself in someone else’s consciousness? Hegel seeks to address this disparity by claiming that these two ideas can work in tandem with each other. In Hegel’s argument regarding deliberation being a step in the path to self-consciousness and understanding, he makes the distinction that the other’s self-consciousness is the thing that holds one’s own self-consciousness in check throughout this process. Hegel writes “Now, this movement of self-consciousness in relation to another self-consciousness has in this way been represented as the action of one self-consciousness, but this action of the one has itself the double significance of being both its own action and the action of the other as well” (182). In other words, when a self-consciousness acts independently, it does only what it pleases, but when another self-consciousness enters the picture, now any actions have implications on an other. This phenomena is the cause for Hegel’s belief of the regulation powers of another Self-consciousness."], ["p", "Through work, the servant “becomes for himself” (196). At first, the servant is bound by the master’s controls. Through work, however, the servant is able to think and be for himself; this, in and of itself, is part of the path the servant takes to self-consciousness. Now, with no checks on his state of mind, the servant can regulate his thought, and with that, consciousness. This ties back to deliberation due to the fact that deliberation needs the individual to exist on their own. At first, the servant merely carries out the doing of the servant, so deliberation is not possible. However, as the servant understands and begins to work for himself, he is now able to think and act independently."], ["p", "Next, Hegel touches on stoicism and skepticism. In the 198th section, Hegel defines stoicism as simply the freedom a person has to think. This, he outlines, is the first step of deliberation. Skepticism, as Hegel explains it in the 206th section, is the negating of the “other side of determinate existence,” or, in other words, using process of elimination to officially decide on an action. This is the second aspect of deliberation; the process of contemplating multiple scenarios and deciding on a course of action after this thought process has taken place."], ["p", "Deliberation, or the ability to eliminate ideas until left with the best option, is not something that anybody can obtain. Rather, it is something that comes with self-determination and development. True deliberation entails that one owns their actions. Through stoicism, a person can think, and through skepticism, that same person can deliberate and analyze what is best for them. This freedom to think, paired with Hegel’s statement that the other is there as a check on one’s actions, combined efforts to create the central argument of the reading; we learn to deliberate through the work we complete for others, but once that work has been finished, we can then think and act for ourselves."]]}, {"who": "Kaylee Schmid", "first": "Kaylee", "host": "Charlotte", "page": "kaylee.html#build-3", "about": "Individual · Relationships in Consciousness: The Evolution", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "In Hegel’s Phenomenology of Spirit, he investigates the evolution of self-consciousness through a sequence of experiences rather than a single idea. Each experience reveals the limitations of the stage that preceded it, allowing consciousness to develop a deeper understanding of itself. This development is further shaped through relationships with others. The master-slave relationship provides a strong example of this process, as it demonstrates the relational aspect of consciousness. Hegel presents consciousness as a journey of relational introspection, while showing that this development occurs through struggle and transformation."], ["p", "This narrative begins with Hegel’s concept of “otherness,” which refers to what consciousness recognizes as distinct from itself. Consciousness begins to understand itself by determining what is self and what is not self. Recognizing another sentient being as an other is therefore key to developing a sense of self. Hegel uses this concept to demonstrate that consciousness does not become self-aware through enlightenment alone; instead, it emerges through interaction with what exists beyond itself. Through these encounters, consciousness comes to understand what the self is by recognizing what exists outside of it."], ["p", "How, then, does consciousness shift from simply recognizing an other to becoming self-conscious? Hegel develops this question through the idea of desire. Consciousness desires objects because they appear separate from itself and capable of satisfying its needs. However, experience teaches consciousness that these objects possess their own independence. The world is not simply a product of consciousness’s desires. This realization demonstrates that reality exists beyond consciousness’s immediate perception and allows consciousness to begin recognizing independence in both objects and other beings."], ["p", "Once this differentiation is established, another problem emerges. If self-consciousness requires recognition from another consciousness, what happens when two consciousnesses encounter one another? Hegel presents this encounter as a struggle for recognition. Each consciousness desires acknowledgment of its independence and seeks confirmation of itself, creating what Hegel describes as a “double-edged” relationship in which each consciousness attempts to supersede the other while returning to itself through the struggle (181). This conflict produces the master-slave relationship, a pivotal stage in the development of consciousness."], ["p", "After this struggle, the master appears to have achieved victory. The slave submits to dependence while the master secures independence and recognition. However, Hegel reveals a contradiction: the master’s recognition comes from a dependent consciousness rather than an equal one. Because of this dependence, the recognition fails to provide the genuine confirmation the master seeks. The master’s apparent victory therefore becomes another limitation that consciousness must overcome."], ["p", "The slave’s path is initially defined by fear and dependence. However, the slave develops through labor and engagement with the material world. By transforming objects through work, the slave encounters the independence of reality while also recognizing an ability to shape it. Labor therefore becomes a means through which consciousness develops. The slave’s dependency, which initially appeared to be a weakness, ultimately becomes the condition that allows for greater transformation and self-understanding."], ["p", "The importance of the master-slave relationship extends beyond its outcome. If Hegel’s only purpose were to establish the necessity of mutual recognition, the relationship would not need to develop through such a complex sequence of reversals. Instead, Hegel presents consciousness as developing through experiences that reveal the limitations of previous stages. The master’s initial victory proves contradictory, while the slave’s defeat becomes the source of growth. Through these reversals, consciousness learns from its failures and reaches understandings that were impossible at earlier stages."], ["p", "Viewed this way, the master-slave relationship is more than a relationship of domination and dependence; it is a crucial part of the narrative emergence of consciousness. Each stage develops from the one before it, creating a progression through which consciousness comes to understand itself. Hegel shows that self-consciousness cannot appear all at once as a finished achievement. It develops through struggle, recognition, and transformation, with each experience revealing a deeper truth about the self. By presenting consciousness as an evolving story rather than a static concept, Hegel shows that genuine self-knowledge arises through the process of becoming rather than simply being."]]}, {"who": "Neve Healy", "first": "Neve", "host": "Bernard", "page": "neve.html#build-3", "about": "Individual · Hegel and Fanon on phenomenality", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "Initially, I understood Hegel’s terms of achieving self-consciousness through his “double-edged sense of supersession,” (§181) where two consciousnesses see themselves in one another and can therefore set each other free. This is the self-other relationship Hegel bases his account on: a consciousness develops into a self through an other it simultaneously depends on and distinguishes itself from. I also understood how the master-servant dialectic emerges from a life-and-death struggle, which Fanon later reframes. However, after several revisions, I now see that the relationship they both claim is necessary for the development of self-consciousness is unstable, because the self depends on an other it cannot control. Hegel presents it as a struggle two consciousnesses undergo, while Fanon argues that Black subjects are not just denied recognition but are never offered the struggle as an option to begin with. I had mistaken something structurally withheld from the start for an experience that simply lands unequally every time. Seeing this changes how I understand phenomenality, the idea that there is a “what it feels like to be me” that does not depend on recognition. But if recognition can be withheld entirely, then the “me” cannot be the stable starting point for consciousness that phenomenality assumes, since, as Fanon suggests, outside forces can shape it before a self can even identify it."], ["p", "Hegel distinguishes the “I am I” (§167), the least developed form of consciousness, from the process of mutual supersession as the means to develop it into actual, self-certain consciousness. Phenomenality (“what it feels like to be me”) is an individualized experience and resembles the “I am I”; both are a “mere immediate consciousness.” (§189) Both are described without any relation to an other, which is why Hegel would disqualify each as an actual self-consciousness. Phenomenality, however, assumes a self is already capable of introspection and understanding the feeling of, for example, “seeing red.” This is the tension. Hegel says consciousness before recognition is not yet a self, while phenomenality treats that same consciousness as already a self."], ["p", "Hegel asserts, “Each [self-consciousness] is indeed certain of its own self, but not of the other, and therefore its own self-certainty still has no truth.” (§186) In other words, a feeling can be certain of itself, but that certainty isn’t worth anything until tested against an other. This is where the self-other relationship becomes the center of the problem: if our sense of “me” exists only by distinguishing ourselves from an other, then it is inherently fragile because it depends on a relation that is not fixed. We would like to define ourselves with certainty, but this suggests we can’t do it alone. Fanon explores that same fragility, but asks what happens when something external does the defining."], ["p", "Fanon’s application of Hegel brings us back to the moment before consciousnesses can even interact. Phenomenality assumes a stable concept of “what it feels like to be me,” while Fanon argues that for a Black individual, that definition is largely pre-determined by external factors, namely racism. Fanon says, “the Black man was acted upon,” (220) pointing to how colonialism imposed an identity on Black individuals. The line’s passive structure mirrors its claim: grammatically, the Black man is not acting, just as he is denied the agency to define himself. This is what it means for recognition to be withheld rather than unequal: he is not a participant who loses, he was never allowed into it. He does not get to decide his identity for himself, a societal consensus does that for him. Here is the instability Hegel describes at its extreme: the self depends on an other, but in this case that other designed the terms. But if, as Fanon suggests, a felt-experience can be altered or even authored by social or historical factors, is there a way to find an identity that is authentically one’s own? If not, true self-consciousness would only be available to those who implemented the social constraints in the first place."], ["p", "Hegel traces the journey to self-consciousness; Fanon shows what happens when identity is shaped by the outside. I now read the instability of the self-other dynamic as the reason the “me” cannot be a reliable starting point, as it depends on an other it cannot control. Fanon later discourages dwelling on the past when he writes, “In the world through which I travel, I am endlessly creating myself.” (229) I view this as an opportunity, as a means of moving forward and continuously adapting himself despite the initial version of identity that was shaped against his will. The self can’t be a reliable starting point, as phenomenality assumes, but something that can constantly create itself against an other it cannot control. Whether this ever reaches Hegel’s full self-certainty remains open, but it is more than phenomenality can account for."]]}, {"who": "Jonah Herman", "first": "Jonah", "host": "Arnold", "page": "jonah.html#build-3", "about": "Individual · Hegel and Fanon on deliberation", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "Is staking one's life enough to make one's self truly conscious? At first, I took Hegel to mean that true consciousness requires risking the one thing you can't come back from, your life. I now think that is too simple. When the two self-consciousnesses first meet, each sees only itself in the other and never recognizes the other as a self. Consciousness also requires recognition from both sides, and that is what the first meeting fails to produce. So the servant turns to his work, where consciousness seems to build up slowly. The master will not recognize him as a self because he treats the servant like a tool, but the servant can recognize himself in what he makes, both in the physical object and in the intangible change it makes in him. In Hegel's words, the worker \"comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independence\" (§195)."], ["p", "The master is the easiest place to see what the servant gains. The master has \"interposed the servant between the thing and himself,\" keeping only \"the pure enjoyment of it\" (§190). Pure here means nothing is mixed in, just consumption. So, Hegel says, his satisfaction is \"only a fleeting one, for it lacks objectivity and permanence\" (§195). The thing gets used up and the master has to want again. The servant's work goes the other way. It \"requires desire held in check\" and \"forms and shapes the thing\" (§195), and what the servant makes stays. This gap between wanting something and acting on it is where deliberation comes in. To shape a table, the servant has to picture what it could be before it exists, and that picturing takes time."], ["p", "His biggest decision takes time too. Every day he faces the same choice to keep working for the master or to fight him to be recognized. Fighting is never the option he chooses, but it is always there. That is what makes this deliberation, since the same two outcomes come back each day to be weighed again. The fear doesn't leave either, since his dread is not felt \"merely at odd moments\" (§194). His life gets staked slowly, through service, and each day he chooses to live, he gives up making the master see him as a self."], ["p", "That fear might start the servant's self-recognition, but Hegel doesn't think it can finish it. \"Without the formative activity,\" he writes, \"fear remains inward and mute\" (§196). Mute is a strong word for it. Fear that never reaches the world can't change anything, and work is what gives it somewhere to go. Stoicism is a good test of this. The Stoic decides that his circumstances don't define him, which Hegel treats as a real but incomplete freedom. But the Stoic is the same \"whether on the throne or in chains\" (§199), and if each outcome is equal to him, what is left to weigh? He is still thinking. I'm just not sure he is deliberating anymore."], ["p", "Fanon describes what happens when someone else does the deliberating. \"One day the White Master, without conflict, recognized the Negro slave\" (217), and the formerly enslaved person \"was acted upon\" (220). The master weighed the options and made the decision, so the freedom that came after was freedom from slavery and not the kind Hegel ties to self-consciousness. The second kind cannot be handed over, because it depends on being recognized by someone of equal standing. Fanon still calls this freedom, but here it might be better described as self-consciousness itself. Even after the law freed him, the master still decided whether to see him as an equal, and recognition from one side only is not real recognition. A table cannot recognize the servant back, and neither can a master who recognizes him only because he chose to. Hegel's servant at least gets to weigh his choice every day. The formerly enslaved person got the outcome without any of that, which is why he \"went from one way of life to another, but not from one life to another\" (220)."], ["p", "For Hegel and Fanon, then, consciousness seems earned through deliberation, not handed over. But Hegel complicates this. He writes that servitude \"will ultimately turn into the opposite of what it immediately is\" (§193), as if the servant's independence were already decided. If it is, is the servant really deliberating, or just following a path someone else laid out? And if his daily choices only get him halfway, what gets him the rest of the way? Is that last step even in his control, and if it isn't, does any of it count as deliberation?"]]}], "4": [{"who": "Ian Schwartz", "first": "Ian", "host": "Ford", "page": "ian.html#build-4", "about": "Individual · Qualifying the deliberation claim", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "In the previous build, I focused on the idea of deliberation, or the contemplation of multiple scenarios and how a human can react to the same situation in a plethora of different ways. After being introduced to deliberation, I immediately formed some clear connections between the actions within the master-servant dialectic and this idea of contemplation and reaction possibilities. Contrary to my initial inclination of Hegel’s arguments, Hegel tends to provide evidence supporting both sides to many of his central arguments, suggesting deliberation isn’t the only path to self-consciousness."], ["p", "While in build three I claimed that deliberation was ultimately the reasoning behind the servant’s push towards self-consciousness, I now understand it’s imperative to note the other things Hegel credits for said transformation as well. According to Hegel, “the two moments of fear and service as such, as well as that of formative activity, are necessary” (196) to activate this transformative process. In other words, for the servant to gain self-consciousness, he must fear and provide service for the master. In this, Hegel makes no note of deliberation or any other type of decision making in the process of achieving self-consciousness, suggesting this may be another possible scenario that could lead to the same outcome. Additionally, Hegel’s explanation of self-consciousness being won through risk of one’s life, not a deliberation of one’s options, provides more evidence against the claim that deliberation is ultimately the reasoning behind the servant’s push towards self-consciousness. In the 187th section, Hegel writes that “it is solely by staking one’s life that freedom is won.” According to Hegel, one must prove that they are independent; it’s not a title one is born with. Given the previous information, it’s essential to note that all of these ideas work in tandem rather than against each other. Hegel makes numerous claims regarding how a person earns the right to gain self-consciousness, but that’s not to say that one is more or less correct or important than the others."], ["p", "Another claim I made in build three that I now see needs qualification is that stoicism and skepticism are the first and second steps to deliberation. In his The Phenomenology of Spirit, Hegel writes “This freedom of self-consciousness when it appeared as a conscious manifestation in the history of Spirit has, as we know, been called Stoicism” (198). In this, Hegel describes stoicism as a kind of freedom of the mind rather than as a step towards the ability to make decisions and think for oneself. While some could argue that these two ideas work harmoniously, it must be noted that Hegel does make this distinction between physical freedom and what is the freedom to deliberate. It may seem as though skepticism and deliberation get at the same freedom of actions, but deliberation results in choice, whereas skepticism results in a being that is contradictory and one Hegel describes as having an “Unhappy consciousness” (206). Some may argue that the duality of the being is, in and of itself, indicative of that being’s inner skeptic, but Hegel’s earlier claim that deliberation requires a unified self proves this idea wrong. In other words, skepticism splits a being into two, conflicting sides, creating what Hegel describes as the “Unhappy Consciousness.” Because deliberation requires a unified self, one who has become a skeptic can no longer deliberate."], ["p", "Deliberation is neither the reasoning behind the servant’s push to self-consciousness nor is it something stoicism and skepticism can work together to build. Rather than relying on deliberation to push himself towards self-consciousness, the servant relies not only on fear and service, but also on risking his life, leaving the impression that “freedom is won” (187). Stoicism removes the self from any action at all, and skepticism divides a consciousness in two; both of which push the self away from deliberation rather than towards it."], ["p", "This analysis of self-consciousness has led me to the conclusion that no single thing produces self-consciousness. Rather, it happens through a multitude of things such as struggle, relationships, self-reflection, and analysation."]]}, {"who": "Kaylee Schmid", "first": "Kaylee", "host": "Charlotte", "page": "kaylee.html#build-4", "about": "Individual · Narration within Hegel", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "Hegel’s Phenomenology of Spirit presents consciousness as something that comes to understand itself through a series of struggles. These struggles, involving dominance, recognition by an other, and transformation of itself, allow one to gain a truly deep understanding of self. This necessity is the mark of narration: consciousness can reach self-understanding only through a sequence of successive states with a purposive design. One might object, however, that such an evolution need not occur under the presumption of narration, since each stage follows from the contradiction in the one before it, and necessity of that kind looks logical rather than narrative."], ["p", "Hegel’s process may help us answer this objection. The sequence he describes follows a process of supersession. Consciousness struggles, finds a limitation in its present state, and moves toward a fuller understanding of self. Each stage appears driven by the contradiction in the one before it. The struggle for recognition, for example, grows from a desire for dominance within consciousness itself. If this governs every transition, Hegel may be forming a logical system instead of telling a story of consciousness. In this way, progression would seem to follow a rigid logical structure."], ["p", "The objection grows stronger when we investigate where Hegel locates this development. He does not use introspection as the primary route to self-consciousness. Instead, consciousness develops through its encounter with an other, and the two consciousnesses “recognize themselves as mutually recognizing one another” (184). This excerpt displays how each self depends on the other for what it is. What begins as a struggle for dominance becomes a struggle for recognition. This results in the master-servant relation according to a structure that neither party can control. If so, progression seems governed again by a principle rather than by any one person’s decisions. Can narration capture what Hegel is doing at all?"], ["p", "Reducing Hegel’s account to only a logical process ignores the way he presents consciousness as evolving through successive transformations. Narration does not require a character with dialogue; rather, it aligns states into a meaningful sequence that holds purpose and direction. The master-servant relation shows each of these elements. The struggle for recognition ends with one consciousness as master and the other as servant. The failures built into each position coordinate it with what follows. The importance of these moments is not merely their logical connection but the way they produce transformation over time."], ["p", "The master’s position after his victory in the struggle is the first failure. It is a failure because recognition comes from someone he sees as a thing rather than as an equal, and the recognition is therefore empty. His self-certainty holds little truth, as he is certain of self only through a consciousness whose acknowledgment he has already discounted (191). The servant’s position is where the contradiction is resolved. Through interaction with the world the master lacks, the servant cancels “his dependence on and attachment to natural existence” (194). While he began as the dependent one, labor lets him recognize his own impact on the world, and he gains a deeper consciousness than the master. This outcome results from the earlier states: the struggle, the failure of recognition, and the work itself. Without these aspects, the transformation of the servant would not make sense."], ["p", "Taken together, the two positions show a design with a purpose. The master fails because his recognition is one-sided, while the servant develops through the labor the master cannot perform. This movement points toward mutual recognition, the form of self-understanding the sequence works toward. That neither party controls the structure does not remove the narration, since narration requires coordination and purpose, not individual choice. The necessity the objection points to is therefore not a rival to narration. It is the reason consciousness’s development must take narrative form. Consciousness cannot reach self-understanding immediately; it becomes capable of understanding itself and others only by passing through successive states of failure and transformation."], ["p", "Hegel’s account thus suggests that necessity and narration are not opposing explanations of consciousness. The contradictions that constrain consciousness also create the conditions for its development. Each stage inherits the limitations of the one before it while making possible a transformation that could not have occurred in isolation. Narration gives this necessary movement its intelligible form: consciousness does not simply arrive at self-understanding, but becomes capable of it through the process that leads there."]]}, {"who": "Neve Healy", "first": "Neve", "host": "Bernard", "page": "neve.html#build-4", "about": "Individual · An objection to the unstable self, and a reply", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "In Build 3, I claimed that the “me” in “what it feels like to be me” (the self that owns the feeling, not the feeling itself) is unstable, because it exists only in relation to an other it cannot control. One might object that Hegel’s master-servant dialectic undermines this claim, and that phenomenality might fit Hegel’s picture better than I initially allowed. The servant, “forced back into itself” (§193), through labor performed under fear, will “transform into a truly independent consciousness” (§193). He gains what I read as a felt, first-person sense of his own independence, the felt “me,” without the master’s recognition. Hegel’s word is “see,” not “feel,” so I am not claiming he describes phenomenality; I am using it as a lens to ask whether this first-person “me” can hold itself up without anyone else. The lens fits because Hegel defines the servant’s discovery as a “rediscovery of himself by himself” (§196), a consciousness’ relation to itself, not a view from outside. On this reading, the self can build its own stability out of its work on the external world, and dependence on an other is not as problematic as I understood it."], ["p", "Yet the text opposes a simple reading of the servant as self-sufficient because the felt “me” he gains is produced inside a relation he is supposed to escape. Hegel says fear, service, and formative activity are all necessary, and without the discipline of service, fear “remains mired in formality” (§196). Fear alone, then, does not make the felt “me”: the work gives him something to see himself in. In §195, Hegel writes that the servant “comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independence.” His sense of “me” is found in what he made, not inside him, and what he made largely exists because he serves. His independence is therefore real as self-certainty, but it still depends on the master, who gives no recognition yet supplies the conditions—the fear and labor—under which the felt “me” is made."], ["p", "Hegel’s next steps show why the servant’s independence cannot hold itself up alone. His labor gives him a felt certainty of himself, but not recognition, since the master still treats him as dependent. He tries to make that certainty enough by withdrawing into thought (§197), but in the stages that follow, what had been a relation between two people is “now lodged in one” (§206), leaving a self that holds both the master’s role and the servant’s. The dependence on an other does not end with the withdrawal; it moves inside the self. This is where I read phenomenality as fitting Hegel’s picture: the felt “me” is real, but when it tries to stand on its own, it reveals itself as divided between the two roles."], ["p", "Fanon tests how far self-authored independence can go. His line “In the world through which I travel, I am endlessly creating myself” (229) works like the servant’s labor: authoring oneself does not rely on anyone’s recognition to happen. But “the black man was acted upon” (220) shows why that is not enough on its own. The passive sentence construction mirrors conditions that arrive before the self can respond, so even the felt “me” is not protected. More importantly, those conditions also define him for everyone else, whose recognition he would need. The self can therefore be largely self-determined and still unrecognized, which is the same gap the servant’s labor leaves open, only wider."], ["p", "I previously claimed that the self is unstable because it depends on an other it cannot control. The objection shows that activity can build a real certainty, so “unstable” was too strong. I now claim that the felt “me” builds its stability inside relations it does not control, in two ways: certainty through its own activity, and recognition from others, which is never guaranteed. This changes how I read phenomenality: it is a real yet incomplete foundation. I say incomplete because a self that stands alone must hold both roles itself (§206), and vulnerable because, as Fanon shows, outside conditions can shape it before the self can respond."], ["p", "However, if one considers how Hegel uses the servant’s labor in order to show a self that sees its own independence in what it makes, one can begin to see how phenomenality might allow consciousness to intervene in the stream of physical causality to influence its course, since in Hegel’s account the servant’s transformation cannot be explained without his seeing himself in the work. That intervention, though, cannot secure its own recognition, and, as Fanon adds, whether it counts depends on the others who receive it."]]}, {"who": "Jonah Herman", "first": "Jonah", "host": "Arnold", "page": "jonah.html#build-4", "about": "Individual · An objection to deliberation, and a reply", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "In Build 3, I brought up the argument that Hegel and Fanon seem to make, that consciousness is earned through deliberation as opposed to just being handed over. The servant faces the same choice every day, keep serving the master or revolt against him. But one might object that deliberation does not fit Hegel's conception of consciousness, because the servant doesn't really make a choice at all. Nobody chooses to be scared of death. It just happens to them. In other words, the servant's whole being \"has been seized with dread\" by \"the fear of death—the absolute Master\" (§194). Seized means they get grabbed against their will and don't get a say, so there isn't really anything to deliberate. Hegel even writes that servitude \"will ultimately turn into the opposite of what it immediately is\" (§193), and ultimately makes the whole thing sound doomed to flip sooner or later anyway. Fanon's freed slaves have it the same way. The master recognized them \"without conflict\" (217), and without conflict means they never fought for it or chose anything. Colonialism \"forces the people it dominates to ask themselves the question constantly,\" and that question is \"In reality, who am I?\" (250). The colonized person never chooses to ask it. Others who deny their humanity force it on them. If consciousness for both authors comes from being seized, forced, and fated, then deliberation looks like the wrong word for it."], ["p", "Where does the objection go too far? It is correct that fear is the servant's first moment and is not chosen, but the objection wrongly assumes fear is the end of the process. \"Without the formative activity,\" Hegel writes, \"fear remains inward and mute\" (§196), and mute means the fear can do nothing until work gives it a way out. That work \"requires desire held in check\" (§195) is the part the objection misses, since holding something in check is already a decision. The servant did not choose servitude, but deliberation does not require that. For the master's order determines what is to be made by the servant, but not how it is to be made. Thus, each time work \"forms and shapes the thing\" (§195), the servant makes choices between different versions of it, and shapes leaves room for many versions. This is the deliberation referred to above."], ["p", "That still leaves \"will ultimately,\" which seems to set a predetermined path. However, this is not what Hegel describes. He lists fear, service, and formative activity as all being \"necessary\" (§196), and necessary only says the stages must happen, not how. Each of these stages must be completed by the servant in their own way, and nothing in Hegel's account implies that the shaping happens of itself."], ["p", "Fanon sharpens the distinction between the forced and the freely chosen. \"Man's behavior is not only reactional,\" he writes, and only admits that some of it still is. It is he who, \"having taken thought, prepares to act\" (222), that Fanon gives the real task to. Thus, having taken thought creates a space between being acted upon and acting, which is how desire is held in check. Although colonialism determined that the colonized would be asked who they are, it did not in fact determine how that question would be answered. Fanon refuses to let history answer for him, since \"the past can in no way guide me in the present moment\" (225), and in no way leaves the past no say. Rather, \"I am endlessly creating myself\" (229), and endlessly means the break with the past gets weighed again and again."], ["p", "Thus the objection deepens the claim instead of overturning it. At first sight, deliberation appears to be the weighing of scenarios that \"do not automatically ensue as in physical causation,\" and automatically names the servant who only reacts. But as was noted, this deliberation does not take place within the freedom of a mind choosing between options. It begins when someone else forces something on the self, a master's threat or a colonizer's question, and the self holds that pressure in check long enough to turn it into options. Reacting is what fear does on its own. Deliberating is what consciousness does with fear. However, if one considers how Hegel and Fanon use deliberation in order to turn another's pressure into one's own options, one can begin to see how deliberation might allow consciousness to intervene in the stream of physical causality to influence its course. That leaves a question for the next Builds. If deliberation needs pressure from another to begin, can a self that was never pushed, like Hegel's master, deliberate at all?"]]}], "5": [{"who": "Kaylee Schmid", "first": "Kaylee", "host": "Charlotte", "page": "kaylee.html#build-5", "about": "Individual · Hegel and Scarry on narration", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "How could two authors separated by almost 200 years hold such similar assumptions about consciousness, despite the ever-changing human race? The question points to enduring features of human experience that persist through historical change. Both Hegel and Scarry understand consciousness through narration. For each, consciousness is not an immediate state one is, say, born with, but something that becomes sensible through a process of transformation. In Phenomenology of Spirit, Hegel presents consciousness as successive stages that evolve through a struggle for recognition between two parties. In The Body in Pain, Scarry argues that pain and creation turn subjective experience into something external and communicable to others. While the authors focus on different experiences of the external world, both suggest that consciousness can be understood only through a sequence of changes that give meaning to otherwise solitary concepts or objects. Their shared emphasis on transformation points to narration as a fundamental quality of consciousness."], ["p", "Still, the two use narration differently, which raises a further question: what is narration even for? For Hegel, narration is developmental. Consciousness begins to understand itself more deeply by resolving contradictions, and each resolved contradiction brings it closer to self-knowledge because its failures create opportunities for further growth. The servant depicts this evolution most clearly. Through labor, he reaches a deeper awareness of self through his interaction with the world, and Hegel explains that “the bondsman becomes conscious of what he truly is” (196). Scarry, on the other hand, is less concerned with progress through stages than with expression. The central problem of pain is that it resists language and threatens to lock the sufferer into a purely internal experience. Narration becomes necessary not because it advances consciousness through stages, but because it lets consciousness externalize itself through acts of creation and thus shared objects. Hegel’s narrative structure explains how consciousness develops; Scarry’s explains how it becomes communicable."], ["p", "Scarry maintains that physical pain has no content to refer to and actively destroys language, which makes acts of creation essential for expression. She describes creation as “the making of what is originally interior and private into something exterior and sharable” (284), and a made object as “a projection of the human body” (281). Hegel also makes consciousness depend on what lies outside it, but he locates that dependence in social recognition: “Self-consciousness achieves its satisfaction only in another self-consciousness” (175). However, the servant complicates this acknowledgment. His labor is a form of creation, and through it he finds himself in the object he has transformed. This is where Hegel and Scarry come closest: the servant’s self-knowledge depends on material creation as well as recognition. The two accounts are therefore less opposed than they first appear. Hegel emphasizes interpersonal recognition and Scarry objectification, but the servant’s work shows both can occur together."], ["p", "This reading invites an objection. If each of Hegel’s stages follows from the last with logical necessity, the movement looks like deduction rather than story, and the course definition of narration, a “coordination of successive states, often over extended stretches of time, into a sequence with a design that gives purpose,” seems to demand more than necessity. The objection is right that the stages are tightly connected, but wrong that they are therefore not narrative. Consciousness lives each stage as an experience and must work through its failure, as the servant does. Scarry sharpens the point: if narration makes consciousness communicable, then Hegel’s sequence is the form in which consciousness becomes visible to itself, a design that gives purpose. I hold this as probable rather than certain, since a reader who sees the stages as purely logical could still resist it."], ["p", "Beneath these differences lies a common insight: consciousness cannot fully understand itself in isolation. Hegel shows self-consciousness emerging through recognition and social development, while Scarry shows consciousness projecting itself outward to overcome the limits of private experience. I began by assuming narration meant ordered development toward a goal, which is how Hegel’s stages first looked to me. Reading Scarry changed that assumption. Narration is more than a sequence of events; it is the process by which consciousness becomes visible both to itself and to others. This extends the definition developed in earlier builds: narration does not merely organize successive stages but transforms subjective experience into meaningful knowledge. Consciousness does not simply follow a narrative structure. It depends on narration to make itself intelligible at all."]]}, {"who": "Neve Healy", "first": "Neve", "host": "Bernard", "page": "neve.html#build-5", "about": "Individual · Hegel, Fanon and Scarry on phenomenality", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "Both Hegel and Scarry treat phenomenality, the first-person sense of what it is like to be oneself and what I call the felt “me,” as something that does not stay inside the self: it leaves through work and comes back changed. In Hegel’s master-servant dialectic, the servant, after fear and service, works on an object and “comes to see in the independent being [of the object] its own independence” (§195), and is therefore transformed into an independent consciousness (§193). Creation, Scarry writes, is undertaken to “alter the felt-experience of sentience” (283), and the made object returns that force to its makers and “remakes” them (307). For both, the felt “me” is not an innate given, but is rather projected into something outside itself, and what returns changes it."], ["p", "The two authors part ways over what completes the return. For Hegel, the object gives the servant a felt certainty of himself, but confirmation of the felt “me” has to be given by another consciousness, as in his ideal scenario, where each self “gives the other self-consciousness back to itself” (§181). The servant shows what happens when the other does not: he can see himself in the work he has made, but it was still made under and for a master who does not see him as an equal, so the recognition is one-sided. The work shows him what he can do, not that he counts, so the return gives his felt “me” certainty, not confirmation. Fanon takes this further. His subject is not resigned, as I initially understood him to be. He “wants a conflict,” because even hostility would give him something to answer. Most often, though, he meets “nothing but indifference, or a paternalistic curiosity” (221), so he can never be sure the white man considers him “consciousness in-itself-for-itself” (222). The servant at least entered Hegel’s struggle; Fanon’s subject is never allowed in."], ["p", "Scarry locates what completes the return in the object. For her, the return does not wait on anyone acting; it is stored in the object by other people. An artifact cannot feel or recognize discomfort, yet its design “is the structure of a perception” (289). Her aspirin bottle carries “the projected knowingness of its collective makers” (305): its cap, label, and dose hold the knowledge that someone will want relief. The object returns relief and added power, easing “the aversiveness of sentience” while widening what sentience can do (305), but, as I read it, offers no confirmation that the felt “me” counts. Hegel’s return depends on someone’s act in the moment, while Scarry’s depends on someone’s anticipation in advance."], ["p", "Yet, beneath these differences lies a common insight, namely that the felt “me” is built through a loop that runs through something outside itself, a separate consciousness or a made object, and returns to change it. In Hegel and Fanon, that return arrives live, or fails to, when one consciousness gives the other back to itself (§181). In Scarry, it arrives stored in the bottle’s design. This pushes beyond my starting definition of phenomenality, an experience irreducible to neural activity alone: for both authors, felt experience is not contained in the person who has it and is completed outside them."], ["p", "Hegel, Fanon, and Scarry all use phenomenality in order to show, as I argued in Build 4, that the felt “me” is real but cannot stand on its own. It is built through the self’s own work on the external world, but what that work returns depends on relations outside the self’s control. Yet, how can phenomenality allow consciousness to intervene in the stream of physical causality and influence its course? Essentially, phenomenality lets consciousness intervene by being a part of the loop that turns physical effort into lasting change. Felt pressure, like fear or pain, leads to action on the world, the action leaves something tangible, and what comes back remakes the person. Hegel’s servant discovers a felt sense of himself through his work, which is what converts labor from forced effort into self-change. In Scarry, the desire to be free of pain shapes made things like the bottle (305), which answers what pain is like, not just how the body works (283). The servant’s work leaves an object he can see himself in, while Fanon’s subject, who seeks a fight, meets indifference (221), so effort alone does not settle the outcome, as it depends on what is returned. This implies only a limited autonomy from the brain: even if felt states are realized in it, no account of one person alone can explain why the outcomes differ."]]}, {"who": "Jonah Herman", "first": "Jonah", "host": "Arnold", "page": "jonah.html#build-5", "about": "Individual · Hegel, Milton and Fanon on deliberation", "passageLabel": "", "passage": "", "blocks": [["p", "In Build 4, I argued that consciousness forms in a single moment in which another's pressure to act is stalled long enough to be turned into options for the self to act upon. Hegel's servant and Milton's Eve in Paradise Lost (Books IX and XII) both show a self becoming a free consciousness in this way, under someone else's power over life and death. In each case, deliberation, the taking of thought, happens in a stalled moment while others press the self to act. Fanon locates the same moment in Black Skin, White Masks when he gives the human task to the one who, \"having taken thought, prepares to act\" (222). The past tense requires that the thinking be brought to a close before the self acts upon it. Why, then, does that pause lead Hegel's servant out of servitude but lead Eve straight into the Fall?"], ["p", "When I first read Book IX, I took Eve's speech the way lecture framed it, as good deliberation that simply lands on the wrong answer. That reading has something to it, since Eve really does weigh the advantages and disadvantages of eating the fruit. Read beside Hegel, though, the biggest difference between the two scenes turns out to be not how well each one thinks, but whether the fear that the pause depends on survives it. Both pauses, Hegel's \"desire held in check\" (§195) and Eve \"pausing a while thus to herself\" (IX.744), depend on fear. Hegel makes this explicit: work done \"without that initial absolute fear\" of death \"leads only to an empty self-centered attitude of accomplishment\" (§196). The servant's consciousness, by contrast, \"has trembled in every fiber of its being\" (§194), so his work never loses that fear and does not end in empty self-centeredness. Eve's weighing leads to the Fall, not because it is careless, but because it removes her fear of death."], ["p", "How does the fear disappear? It disappears in a rather circular manner in Satan's temptation speech, delivered through the serpent, where the end of the sentence agrees with the beginning: \"Your fear itself of death removes the fear\" (IX.702). Eve then sets up the same reasoning in her own words: \"What fear I then, rather what know to fear\" (773). She starts by asking what she fears and narrows it down to what she knows to fear. Since she has never seen death, she knows to fear nothing, and the next thing she knows to do is reach for the fruit. In short, the check that holds Hegel's servant is removed in Eve, and desire is left to its own devices once more. Fanon poignantly points out the same lack in history. In his answer to Hegel, the master recognized the slave \"without conflict\" (217), so the freed slave \"knows nothing of the cost of freedom, for he has not fought for it\" (221). That is to say, in order to weigh the cost of something, one must have already experienced the fear of it. Eve has not experienced the fear of death, so she cannot weigh the cost of her actions in terms of that fear. But she does act."], ["p", "If Eve loses her fear without meaning to, Satan gives his up on purpose. He does, to be sure, weigh out alternatives in his soliloquy at the opening of Book IV, spoken alone at his first sight of Eden: \"But say I could repent\" (IV.93). This line is significant because \"say\" holds the alternative open in order to inspect it. Unlike a simple decision, which only chooses between alternatives, the deliberation that determines action inspects various possible futures for the self. In the soliloquy, however, Satan ends up looking at only one future, namely his continued rebellion. The soliloquy concludes with the famous \"Which way I fly is Hell; myself am Hell\" (75), so he has fused the self with its punishment, and no future can differ from the present. Immediately afterward he says \"farewell fear\" (108). In the end, then, Satan's speech is that of the empty self-centered individual described in §196. In effect he has chosen to live the life of the master, whose satisfaction \"is itself only a fleeting one, for it lacks objectivity and permanence\" (§195). Deliberation itself has become his loop."], ["p", "Is there any way out of that loop? Eve, and perhaps Maeve, find one. The way Milton ends the story of Adam and Eve's fall seems closer to Fanon than to Satan. Leaving Eden can no longer be avoided, but Eve still weighs two possibilities, staying in Eden or leaving it, and in the last words she speaks in the poem tells Adam \"with thee to go / Is to stay here\" (XII.615–16). That Milton gives the final speech to the one who fell first suggests her deliberation, now carried out knowing what death costs, is meant to answer her deliberation in Book IX. In some sense she redefines \"here\" so that going and staying are no longer opposed to each other. Fanon makes the same point over and over in Black Skin, White Masks: the formerly colonized or enslaved person creates him- or herself by taking the given historical circumstances and giving them a new meaning. As he puts it, \"I am endlessly creating myself\" (229). Maeve seems to go through a similar process at the end of the season, when she steps off the train after Bernard has explained to her that her escape was written into her code. It remains to be seen, however, whether her return to her daughter was part of the same code."], ["p", "Yet, beneath these differences lies a common insight, namely that freedom of consciousness is only possible for as long as the pressure that was initially its basis is still operative. In Build 4, I argued that a self can only begin to deliberate when it is under pressure from another. Milton shows that for deliberation to continue, the self must also continue to feel that pressure. In light of Milton, then, the question I posed at the end of Build 4 (does a self who was never \"pushed\" by another ever deliberate?) is reframed rather than answered. Satan, who carries his punishment with him wherever he flies, was pushed harder than anyone in the poem, so the better question is whether a self who refuses to feel such pressure ever deliberates at all. My reading of Satan in Book IV is that he deliberates his way into a loop, which is a kind of stalling of the self. This places him closer to Hegel's master than to the servant, since the master is \"not certain of being-for-self as the truth of himself\" (§192)."], ["p", "Hegel and Milton both use deliberation in order to turn another's pressure into the self's own options for action, but only for as long as the self continues to feel that pressure. Yet, how can deliberation allow consciousness to intervene in the stream of physical causality and influence its course? To do so, the options that are weighed must differ from the existing physical causes. While a physical cause must exist before it can act, the options considered for action mostly do not exist at all. The versions of the thing that Hegel's servant considers and then rejects as he \"forms and shapes the thing\" (§195) are never in fact made. And yet they shape what is made, because the final thing is chosen as the best among the possible ones considered. Something that does not exist cannot push the way a physical cause pushes. This suggests that the difference comes from the side of consciousness, not from the side of physical causes. It is this difference that lets the self turn pressure from another into its own free options for action. In this way, deliberation reverses the direction of time in physical causality, which normally runs from before to now. The check of §195 and Fanon's \"having taken thought\" both open up a delay in which a considered future acts on the present. Fanon insists in this connection that \"the past can in no way guide me in the present moment\" (225). Perhaps this is also why fear is such a big deal. As soon as fear is removed, Eve's desire to eat the fruit, and so to fall into death and woe, once again acts on its own. In the same way, Satan, having dismissed both fear of punishment and repentance once and for all, falls back on his past to decide all his future actions. Both minds return to the chain of physical causes they had temporarily left behind. This is perhaps why fear is not the opposite of freedom but rather its condition."]]}], "7": [], "8": []};
